; Import-linter contracts for the repository layout in docs/LEDGEX_SPEC.md
; §2. Part of `make check-boundary` (I1, I15).
;
; core/ and commerce/ are empty scaffolds (see their __init__.py) --
; created only so lint-imports has a real package to use as a
; source_modules entry; a not-yet-existing package can be named as a
; forbidden_modules target (external reference) but not as a source,
; confirmed directly while writing this file. These two contracts are
; genuinely near-vacuous right now -- there's no code under core/ or
; commerce/ yet for either to catch -- but they are REAL contracts that
; DO run and DO fail the moment a violating import lands, not aspirational
; text waiting for infrastructure to exist.
;
; import-linter is the import-graph half only. Neither contract below
; can see a bare jurisdiction-id string literal with no import attached
; (I1's other half -- see build/check_jurisdiction_names.py) or a database
; grant/schema-separation fact (I15's other half -- the "catalogue query"
; named in the invariant table, not yet built).
;
; jurisdictions/, pipelines/, geo/ don't exist either -- referenced below
; only as forbidden_modules (external), never as a source, so they need no
; scaffold of their own.
;
; api/ P40: now real (api/__init__.py, api/main.py -- the P40 internal
; viewer, NOT §4's customer-facing api/, but a real importable package
; either way). Added to root_packages and to the layers contract below --
; before this, api/'s own imports were completely invisible to
; import-linter (a source_modules gap, not just a documentation one:
; `lint-imports` analyzed the identical file/dependency count with api/
; present as without it, confirmed directly while making this change, not
; assumed). Deliberately NOT given an I15-style "commerce may import
; core.model/core.compose only" allowlist of its own: that shape is
; exactly finding #45's stale-blacklist problem (prompts/
; P40-internal-viewer.md §0) -- a per-submodule forbidden list that already
; silently stopped covering commerce's real constraint three core/
; submodules ago. Adding a second instance of the same broken shape for
; api/ compounds the finding this package found rather than avoiding it;
; the layers contract below still correctly governs api/'s relationship to
; infra/, which is the one directional rule that contract has ever stated.

[importlinter]
root_packages =
    core
    commerce
    infra
    api
include_external_packages = True

[importlinter:contract:i1-core-no-jurisdiction-imports]
name = I1: core must not import jurisdictions, api, pipelines, geo, or commerce
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    core
forbidden_modules =
    jurisdictions
    api
    pipelines
    geo
    commerce

; P47 (README finding #45, closed): this used to forbid core.store alone,
; which was a faithful proxy for §2's real rule ("commerce may import
; core.model and core.compose ONLY") only as long as core/ had nothing else
; under it. It stopped being faithful three packages before anyone
; revisited it (P21 core/exceptions.py, P25 core/calc.py, P31 core/rules.py)
; -- proven live, not merely argued: with the OLD contract in place, a
; planted `commerce -> core.exceptions` import stayed KEPT (this contract
; alone caught nothing; only the separate layers contract below happened to
; catch it, and only as an accidental side effect of forbidding commerce
; from importing core.model too, which §2 explicitly permits -- see that
; contract's own comment for the matching fix).
;
; Genuinely an allowlist now, not a blacklist naming every current core/
; submodule (which breaks again, silently, the next time one is added --
; the exact failure mode being fixed here, and a strong argument against
; repeating its shape). `forbidden_modules = core.*` is import-linter's
; wildcard for "every direct submodule of core," so this forbids the
; WHOLE of core/ from commerce, then `ignore_imports` carves the two real
; exceptions back out -- a genuine allowlist, expressed as "forbid
; everything, then un-forbid exactly what §2 permits," which is the only
; allowlist shape import-linter's "forbidden" contract type supports.
; core.compose is listed even though it does not exist as a file yet
; (§2's compose/ L8 layer is still unbuilt) -- confirmed this does not
; error (unmatched_ignore_imports_alerting = none tolerates an
; ignore_imports entry matching nothing in the graph, which is also why it
; is needed at all today: commerce/ is still an empty scaffold, so
; `commerce.** -> core.model` matches nothing in the graph EITHER, and
; without this setting lint-imports refuses to run at all
; ("No matches for ignored import"), not merely warns.
[importlinter:contract:i15-commerce-core-allowlist]
name = I15: commerce may import core.model and core.compose only
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    commerce
forbidden_modules =
    core.*
ignore_imports =
    commerce.** -> core.model
    commerce.** -> core.compose
unmatched_ignore_imports_alerting = none

[importlinter:contract:i15-core-no-commerce]
name = I15: core must never import commerce (reverse direction)
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    core
forbidden_modules =
    commerce

[importlinter:contract:infra-is-a-leaf]
name = infra must import only stdlib/third-party -- not even core.model
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    infra
forbidden_modules =
    core
    commerce
    jurisdictions
    api
    pipelines
    geo

; P22/finding #29: §2 self-contradicted on whether core/ may import infra/
; -- one bullet said "core/* may import core/model and stdlib/third-party
; only" (predates infra/'s existence: written at v1.7, infra/ introduced at
; v1.21, confirmed via git history, that older line simply never updated),
; another bullet and the repo-layout diagram both said "any of core/,
; commerce/, jurisdictions/, pipelines/, api/ may import infra/". Amended
; in prose to the latter (1.38) -- this is that decision's mechanical
; enforcement, not just documentation. A "layers" contract, not another
; "forbidden" one: it is the one shape here that states BOTH directions
; correctly in a single contract -- core/commerce (independent of each
; other, "|") sit above infra/, meaning they MAY import it; infra/ sits
; below and may never import either, which the forbidden contract above
; already enforced but this restates as part of the same, explicit,
; two-sided rule. jurisdictions/pipelines aren't real packages yet (see
; this file's own header) so aren't listed as layers -- only
; forbidden_modules targets can name a not-yet-existing package.
;
; api/ ADDED P40 (D4): a third, topmost layer, above core|commerce -- §2:
; "api/ FastAPI over core.compose and commerce", and the import-rules bullet
; list already named api/ among the packages allowed to import infra/.
; import-linter's "layers" contract allows a higher layer to import ANY
; lower layer, not only the adjacent one, so this one addition also states
; "api may import infra" without a second contract -- confirmed by running
; lint-imports with a real api -> infra.env import present (this package's
; own api/main.py) and it stays KEPT. Same-level siblings (core | commerce)
; still may not import each other -- EXCEPT the two §2 explicitly permits,
; carved out by ignore_imports below (P47, README finding #45, closed): a
; "layers" contract with core and commerce declared as co-equal siblings
; forbids ANY import between them, which is stricter than §2's real rule
; and was itself part of the finding -- proven live, not merely argued:
; before this fix, a planted `commerce -> core.model` import (spec-
; PERMITTED) made this contract go BROKEN, naming that import as
; disallowed and directly contradicting §2's own text. Fixed here rather
; than by restructuring the layers contract: this contract's real job is
; the infra-layering rule (api/core/commerce may import infra; infra may
; import none of them), which core|commerce-as-siblings is a side effect
; of, not the point -- the commerce/core relationship itself is now fully
; and correctly governed by i15-commerce-core-allowlist and
; i15-core-no-commerce above, so this contract only needs to stop
; contradicting them, not take over their job. Same reasoning and same
; unmatched_ignore_imports_alerting = none as that contract, for the same
; reason: commerce/ has no real code yet.
[importlinter:contract:core-commerce-layers-above-infra]
name = api above core/commerce above infra (finding #29, extended P40 D4)
type = layers
layers =
    api
    core | commerce
    infra
ignore_imports =
    commerce.** -> core.model
    commerce.** -> core.compose
unmatched_ignore_imports_alerting = none
